A||||12||FieroBUZZ|| Solid Cradle Mounting ?? Advice Pls!||||5||
Z||000000||FieroBUZZ||09-07-2001||11:04 AM||||I'll have my cradle off my 86 SE.  I have seen some talk of welding metal in so that the cradle is mounted without a rubber or poly donut.  I can see the benefit in racing, but is this viable on a street car. <P>I don't mind extra vibration and it is a 'fun' car not the family daily driver.<P>Gary||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||09-07-2001||11:20 AM||||I installed some aluminum solid bushings in my 84 last year.  I got them from 1fst2m6 (Travis).  There was no noticable increase in vibration or noise, just a better feel to the rear suspension---it is a little tighter, especially around corners on a rough road.  I wouldn't hesitate to install them on a daily driver.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||09-07-2001||11:47 AM||||88's don't even have cradel bushings do they? I've heard that if the bushings on the cradel get worn, it can cause bad handeling/mis-alignment. Why was it they were even used then??<P>EDIT TYPOS<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||jelly2m8||09-07-2001||12:01 PM||||Shouldn't be a problem mounting the cradle solid to the car, as 88's are mounted solid.<P>On my V8 I have the engine and transaxle mounted solid to the cradle, and I have no extra vibration, theres actually less than when it had rubber transaxle mounts.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierohoho||09-07-2001||01:10 PM||||Youi can order aluminium rear bushings from the Minnesota Fieros Forever web site, not sure what they cost but they do make a handling difference.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pennockpic.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||09-07-2001||01:28 PM||||i've got one set left. $175 shipped. included are the front 2 and the rear 4. I love mine.. i've installed a few poly sets.. and a few aluminum sets but the aluminum units are the nicest so far... they'll out last the car, they are shiney (everyone likes shiney) and help to transfer torque to the ground instead of loosing it to old nasty rubber bushings that just collapse and add to the nasty toe in on acceleration. if your nto getting into CRAZY hp or aren't gunna keep the car for ever and a day just get the Prothane Poly bushing kit from <A HREF="http://www.cyberauto.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cyberauto.com</A>  part numb 7-502. same with NOPI (yep their in Canada too) sold under parent company DeeZee engineering (the old bug people aka Bug pak) they aren't easy to change, but worth the blood, sweat and new vocabulary you'll make up. change thefront first.. the back only take 10 min... the front about 35min to an hour per side. you'll need a bushing press and a butane torch.   ||||reg||1
Z||000006||c m west||09-07-2001||03:20 PM||||we sell 6061 T6 heat treated aluminum cradle bushings for $200, instructions and installation tool included<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-07-2001||07:49 PM||||I covered this some in my bushing article.<P>I did polly on mine. no noticable vibration dif, and sinse cradle doesn't move any significant amount they don't squeek either. I didn't even grease mine when I put them in last year.<P>Polly is less expensive than Al. That's the big reason I used it. It's stiff enough for stock motors. If you have a built up motor or plan on getting one then do the Al.<P>Which ever way you go, be easy on the car for a couple days, a week is better. The bushings don't need break in... It will take you that long to get used to the difference in handling.<P>You'll probably also need an alignment. A tiny change in cradle position can have a major impact on alignment.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Immortal||09-08-2001||01:40 AM||||People if your paying $175 to $200 for alum. bushing I have a bridge to sell you. Aluminum in time mushrooms and does not return. 6061 T6 is run of the mill aluminum. If they are heat treated just makes them brittle. For that price I would went 7071. Your cradle needs that flex. If I put you in three cars set up the exact same one with rubber, one with Polly and one with aluminum you could not tell the difference||||reg||1
Z||000009||c m west||09-08-2001||07:49 PM||||better listen up, sounds like this guy must have designed the Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||09-08-2001||09:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Immortal:<BR><B>People if your paying $175 to $200 for alum. bushing I have a bridge to sell you. Aluminum in time mushrooms and does not return. 6061 T6 is run of the mill aluminum. If they are heat treated just makes them brittle. For that price I would went 7071. Your cradle needs that flex. If I put you in three cars set up the exact same one with rubber, one with Polly and one with aluminum you could not tell the difference</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>B.S.<P>I put in a set of home grown aluminum bushings and was rewarded with a dramatic increase in the ease with which I could hang the rear end out/drift the car/control the lift-off oversteer reaction.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||09-09-2001||04:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR>[B(everyone likes shiney) [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh yes, shiney rules!!!!!<P>Seriously, all 88s have solidly mounted cradles, adn they're better off for it.   I'd go with the aliminum in a heartbeat if you're going to bother to change them at all (real PITA, never again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Another thing to consider is swapping in a whole rear 88 cradle (and suspension.) <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports" TARGET=_blank>http://www.heldmotorsports</A>  has a couple little adapter brackets to make this happen (or you can make your own.)<P>Soon I will have an *entirely* solidly mounted car, the cradle, engine/tranny, AND suspension (via Spherical rod beariong ends.) on 88 geometry.  I'll be posting like a madman once it's all put together, belive you me!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000012||jelly2m8||09-09-2001||06:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<P>Soon I will have an *entirely* solidly mounted car, the cradle, engine/tranny, AND suspension (via Spherical rod beariong ends.) on 88 geometry.  I'll be posting like a madman once it's all put together, belive you me!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> Is this on The V8 car? If so You will like it. There is almost no slack in the driveline this way, and you will  be rewarded with instantaneous ( sp) throttle response. <P> I have changed a few V8's to solid engine/tranny to cradle mounts, and the results are excellent. Next I'll try solid cradle to car mounts... should be nice to feel those big pistons mashin up and down back there...... <P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
